WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
Elena enrolls in life coverage through her employer.

2
00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.840
The employer or qualifying trustees hold the master contract.

3
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.860
Elena receives a certificate explaining her individual protection and rights.

4
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.720
The group policyholder, insured employee, and beneficiary perform different

5
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.580
roles. An employer group covers eligible employees or defined classes under

6
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.160
the policy. A labor union can instead hold coverage for eligible

7
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.080
members. Maryland's rules for these arrangements direct benefits to people

8
00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.560
other than the employer or union, as applicable.

9
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.620
Do not confuse employee group protection with a business owning key

10
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.200
person insurance for its own financial loss.

11
00:00:36.498 --> 00:00:41.358
A trust can hold group coverage for participating employers or unions

12
00:00:41.358 --> 00:00:46.898
under Maryland's rules. The trust or trustees become the policyholder.

13
00:00:47.618 --> 00:00:52.338
A qualifying association can also sponsor coverage, but merely gathering

14
00:00:52.338 --> 00:00:56.778
names to buy insurance does not satisfy the association requirements.

15
00:00:57.718 --> 00:01:02.378
A debtor group is different again: the creditor or another authorized

16
00:01:02.378 --> 00:01:06.958
holder insures eligible debtors in connection with their obligations.

17
00:01:08.338 --> 00:01:13.138
The payment addresses the insured debt, with any payable excess handled

18
00:01:13.138 --> 00:01:14.678
under the applicable rule.

19
00:01:15.798 --> 00:01:20.278
Identify the legal group and its purpose before applying an employer's

20
00:01:20.278 --> 00:01:23.238
eligibility or conversion provision to it.

21
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:29.860
An insurer evaluates the group's characteristics, eligibility rules and

22
00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:35.180
participation. Stable membership and defined benefit classes can

23
00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.460
reduce the chance that only people expecting a near-term claim enroll.

24
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.840
That is a selection issue, not a promise that no individual

25
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.100
ever needs evidence of insurability.

26
00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.680
A late entrant or a person requesting an amount above a

27
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.640
stated evidence-free limit may face additional requirements under the actual

28
00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:01.280
plan. Maryland also permits specified individual insurability exclusions

29
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.180
or limits in these group statutes.

30
00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.420
Read the enrollment window and requested amount instead of answering every

31
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.640
group question with no medical evidence.

32
00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:16.270
If Elena contributes toward her insurance premium, the plan is contributory.

33
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:21.630
If the employer pays the full premium, it is noncontributory.

34
00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:27.470
Maryland's employer rule generally covers all eligible employees in that

35
00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:32.710
noncontributory arrangement, subject to written rejections and its stated

36
00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:37.950
insurability exception. Funding alone does not make the employer

37
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:43.010
the death beneficiary. Benefits ordinarily follow the insured's

38
00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.850
designation and the policy's applicable payment provisions.

39
00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:51.930
Eligible dependents may have separate coverage, subject to the governing

40
00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.010
amount and eligibility limits.

41
00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:58.950
A spouse listed only as beneficiary is not thereby an insured

42
00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:02.648
spouse. Marcus leaves covered employment.

43
00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:08.128
Under the ordinary Maryland group conversion provision, an eligible person

44
00:03:08.128 --> 00:03:12.488
applies and pays the first premium within thirty-one days, subject to

45
00:03:12.488 --> 00:03:18.200
the notice-extension rules. New evidence of insurability is not required.

46
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.568
The individual form, amount and premium follow the statute and

47
00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:27.728
available contract terms. Conversion is different from portability, which

48
00:03:27.728 --> 00:03:32.068
continues coverage under a specific group arrangement if that option exists.

49
00:03:32.068 --> 00:03:37.628
Ending the entire group policy also invokes a separate conversion provision.

50
00:03:37.628 --> 00:03:42.450
Revisit Part sixteen for those amount limits and notice rules.

51
00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.788
Do not assume the former group price continues forever.

52
00:03:46.818 --> 00:03:51.818
Federal Employees' Group Life Insurance, or FEGLI,

53
00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:56.758
provides group term coverage for eligible federal employees, with Basic

54
00:03:56.758 --> 00:03:59.178
coverage and elected optional coverage.

55
00:03:59.898 --> 00:04:01.538
It does not build a cash value.

56
00:04:02.398 --> 00:04:07.598
Servicemembers' Group Life Insurance, or SGLI, provides term coverage to

57
00:04:07.598 --> 00:04:12.698
eligible service members. Veterans' Group Life Insurance, or VGLI,

58
00:04:13.078 --> 00:04:16.718
can continue term protection after qualifying military service when its

59
00:04:16.718 --> 00:04:18.998
enrollment and premium requirements are met.

60
00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:23.198
These are different programs with their own eligibility, deadlines and

61
00:04:23.198 --> 00:04:26.978
benefit schedules. Use the current government guidance for an actual

62
00:04:26.978 --> 00:04:30.798
enrollment. Do not replace a federal program's rules with a generic

63
00:04:30.798 --> 00:04:32.878
Maryland employer conversion deadline.
